  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 233 PM EDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nMonday - Monday Night...\n\nWe'll keep shallow amplitude NW flow aloft in place on the NE \nperiphery of the upper ridge through much of the upcoming work week. \nExpect isolated to scattered shower and storm chances in the \nforecast for Monday as a surface cold front trailing from a weak New \nEngland surface low slides south across our CWA. There may be just \nenough overlap between modest deep layer flow yielding 25-30 knots \nof 0-6 km shear, and moderate instability characterized by 1500-2500 \nJ/KG of ML CAPE. However, the best shear will be farther north, and \nwith the front sliding SE through the day, it appears any window for \nthat overlap would be pretty short. Just a general t-storm risk on \nthe current Day 3 Outlook, and that seems reasonable for now. Any \nbetter overlap timing between the two may end up yielding a Marginal \nRisk so will keep an eye on that trend.\n\nThe frontal boundary sinking south will start to provide some relief \nfrom the heat and humidity across our northern CWA through the day. \nIt will still be hot and humid across southern KY on Monday ahead of \nthe front, and will be looking for low 90s for highs along with max \nheat indices approaching 100F. Monday night should trend dry with \nthe loss of heating, with lows settling into a more comfortable 65 \nto 70 degree range.\n\nTuesday - Wednesday Afternoon...\n\nThe upper ridge will retrograde to the SW CONUS through mid week, \nallowing slight more established NW flow over our area. Doesn't look \nto be a lot of notable shortwave activity embedded in the flow \nthrough mid week, and this combined with surface high pressure \nbuilding in behind the passing cool front we should see mostly dry \nconditions through Wednesday. Highs should mostly range between 85 \nto 90 each day, although southern KY could reach the low 90s again \nby Wednesday. Makes sense being closer to the upper ridge.\n\nWednesday Evening - Saturday...\n\nShower and storm activity looks to pick up again as early as \nWednesday evening, and through much of the late week, as a cool \nfront trailing from an anomalously strong eastern Canada system \ndrops south into our area. In addition, models generally agree in \nincreased shortwave activity embedded with in the NW flow aloft \nstill in place over our area. With multiple triggers in place, we'll \ncarry shower and storm chances through late week into the weekend. \nThe shortwaves should help to continually reinforce slightly cooler \nair across the region, and temps will run below normal for this time \nof year in the low and mid 80s for much of this time. It is possible \nthat troughing gets established well enough by Saturday that more of \nthe convective activity is off to our south and east and we stay \ndrier, however confidence is low given model variability for now.",
